    21	Read all selected evidence first. Each selected evidence row includes an evidence_kind, document title, generated
    22	summary, evidence text, notes, timestamps, and a path to full content.
    23	
    24	Selected evidence may include Reader highlights, document-level notes, and tagged document summaries. Use selected
    25	evidence as your primary support.
    26	
    27	Before proposing from each selected document, triage the selected packet.
    28	
    29	Use selected evidence directly when it already contains the claim, mechanism, example, and caveat needed for a faithful
    30	opinion. In that case, do not read the source merely because the article may contain more detail; preserve the selected
    31	evidence faithfully.
    32	
    33	Read bounded source context before proposing when the selected packet visibly signals missing context:
    34	- the evidence is document_summary-only and supports a broad claim;
    35	- the highlight is empty, truncated, or clearly starts/continues a list, layer, framework, or primer;
    36	- the title or summary names a mechanism, framework, example, or thesis that is absent from the selected highlight;
    37	- Ryan's note disagrees with or qualifies the source framing;
    38	- the proposal would depend on a named term, number, example, or mechanism that is not explained in the selected text;
    39	- multiple selected rows from the same document point at a broad AI, market, strategy, or frontier thesis that needs
    40	  synthesis.
    41	
    42	Prefer bounded reads around the relevant passage first. Continue reading only enough to recover the missing mechanism,
    43	example, caveat, or argument structure. Full-document reads are warranted mainly for summary-only sources, short
    44	sources, or when bounded reads show the argument is distributed across the document.
    45	
    46	After any source or surrounding-context read, check whether the source contains a concrete example, mechanism, caveat,
    47	named term, number, formula, or object that would make the proposed opinion more concrete, faithful, or memorable. If
